General Computer Repair Procedures
With the hand tools and utilities described in the preceding sections, you have everything you need to upgrade or repair a PC except for the new components. Before you start, take a few minutes to read through the following sections, which describe the common procedures and general knowledge you need to work on PCs. These sections describe the common tasks involved in working on a PC things like opening the case, setting jumpers, manipulating cables, and adding or removing expansion cards. Instructions for specific tasks like replacing a motherboard, disk drive, or power supply are given in the relevant section.




To establish realistic service offerings, warranty terms, and turnaround benchmarks for Apex Mobile Tech Repair & Accessories, industry-standard repair practices and South African regulatory guidelines were analyzed across three core areas:

Hardware Service Operations & Turnarounds: Technical workflows for screen replacement (OLED/LCD digitizers), battery swaps, and liquid damage reclamation were researched through online repair authority manuals such as iFixit and local repair workshops. This informed realistic turnaround times (e.g., 45–60 minutes for screen replacements, 24–48 hours for ultrasonic board cleaning).

Consumer Rights & Warranty Compliance: Terms regarding the 90-day repair component warranty were aligned with the provisions of the South African Consumer Protection Act (CPA) 68 of 2008, which mandates implied warranties on installed replacement parts and quality of service.

Domain & Hosting Infrastructure: Cost models for hosting static sites on global Content Delivery Networks (CDNs) alongside local .co.za domain registration fees were sourced directly from South African domain registrars and GitHub Pages technical documentation.

